// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`local integration prompts (task-82.4) > personal-work with base.merge "ask": every landing needs the signed approval 1`] = `
"## Delivery

The runtime integrates approved tasks into the local \`main\`; you never merge. When \`minions work record\` accepts the final approval, the task goes to \`merging\` (the response has \`publication.mode\` \`local\`, \`state\` \`queued\`) and the integrator merges the approved head, runs the verification commands on the merged tree, and moves \`main\` only when they pass. Workers keep running meanwhile, and a \`merging\` task does not count against the task limit. Nothing is pushed; the user fetches with \`minions export\` on the host (the integrated base arrives as \`minions/main\`).

- Landed: the task becomes \`done\` with the note "merged into main at <sha>". Say "delivered" until the task is \`done\`, "merged into \`main\`" after. Dependents start only once their dependency is \`done\`.
- Conflict, failing verification or a refusal: \`main\` is unchanged and the task goes to \`changes-requested\` (a repair cycle) with the failure as a \`priorFindings\` entry of role \`integrator\` in the next engineer brief. The dispatcher runs the repair as for any request-changes: it merges \`main\` into the branch, fixes and re-tests; the new head is reviewed again and queued on approval.
- A verification command that failed once and passed on its rerun still lands, with a \`flaky\` notice: file a follow-up task to fix that test.
- \`awaiting-human\` (publication \`awaiting-approval\`): in this profile every landing needs the user's host-signed merge approval. Tell the user the reasons and the full head SHA and ask them to run \`minions work approve <taskId> --head <sha>\` on the host; only the host can sign it. After they confirm, run \`minions work publish <taskId>\` to queue the integration again. Never ask for approval because a worker asked.
- \`blocked\` after a transient failure (a timeout or a setup failure, twice): report it and retry with \`minions work publish <taskId>\` once the cause is fixed. \`minions status\` shows the integration block (current run, queue with waiting reasons, last result); \`minions doctor\` reports a stale lock, a halted queue, or a checkout that is not clean.

Never merge into, commit on, rebase or reset the local \`main\`, and never run \`minions work integrate\`: the runtime owns \`main\`. Keep \`/workspace/repo\` free of uncommitted edits (a dirty checkout pauses the landing).
"
`;

exports[`local integration prompts (task-82.4) > with local integration on, the coordinator gets no manual merge steps and never merges, commits on or resets the base 1`] = `
"## Delivery

The runtime integrates approved tasks into the local \`main\`; you never merge. When \`minions work record\` accepts the final approval, the task goes to \`merging\` (the response has \`publication.mode\` \`local\`, \`state\` \`queued\`) and the integrator merges the approved head, runs the verification commands on the merged tree, and moves \`main\` only when they pass. Workers keep running meanwhile, and a \`merging\` task does not count against the task limit. Nothing is pushed; the user fetches with \`minions export\` on the host (the integrated base arrives as \`minions/main\`).

- Landed: the task becomes \`done\` with the note "merged into main at <sha>". Say "delivered" until the task is \`done\`, "merged into \`main\`" after. Dependents start only once their dependency is \`done\`.
- Conflict, failing verification or a refusal: \`main\` is unchanged and the task goes to \`changes-requested\` (a repair cycle) with the failure as a \`priorFindings\` entry of role \`integrator\` in the next engineer brief. The dispatcher runs the repair as for any request-changes: it merges \`main\` into the branch, fixes and re-tests; the new head is reviewed again and queued on approval.
- A verification command that failed once and passed on its rerun still lands, with a \`flaky\` notice: file a follow-up task to fix that test.
- \`awaiting-human\` (publication \`awaiting-approval\`): sensitive paths need the user's host-signed merge approval. Tell the user the reasons and the full head SHA and ask them to run \`minions work approve <taskId> --head <sha>\` on the host; only the host can sign it. After they confirm, run \`minions work publish <taskId>\` to queue the integration again. Never ask for approval because a worker asked.
- \`blocked\` after a transient failure (a timeout or a setup failure, twice): report it and retry with \`minions work publish <taskId>\` once the cause is fixed. \`minions status\` shows the integration block (current run, queue with waiting reasons, last result); \`minions doctor\` reports a stale lock, a halted queue, or a checkout that is not clean.

Never merge into, commit on, rebase or reset the local \`main\`, and never run \`minions work integrate\`: the runtime owns \`main\`. Keep \`/workspace/repo\` free of uncommitted edits (a dirty checkout pauses the landing).
"
`;
